  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA stacked closed low pressure system was located over south-central \nKS and north-central OK at 09Z today. The initial round of rain east \nof the cold front and trough axis was exiting the area to the northeast,\nwhile showers and isolated storms were filling in across north- \ncentral KS in the wrap-around region of the system. Additional \nscattered showers and storms are expected to develop due to large \nscale ascent as the low moves over northeast KS this morning. Around \n500 J/kg of MUCAPE could allow for some lightning but severe weather\nis not expected today. Winds increase out of the northwest as the \nlow pulls away due to a tight pressure gradient on the back side of \nthe system. Gusts of 30 to 35 kts could occur, especially in north- \ncentral KS. \n\nA CAA pattern will bring in cooler and drier conditions tomorrow. \nCloud cover should also clear from southwest to northeast throughout \nthe day as temperatures in the mid 40s early warm to near 60 degrees \nduring the afternoon. A mid-level trough axis around the departing \nlow phases with another approaching H5 low over KS on Thursday. An \nassociated sfc low will move along the Red River Valley with an area \nof rain developing in OK and southern KS. The best chance for rain \nwill remain south of I-35 with most other locations likely to stay \ndry on Thursday. Temps stay very close to average as a sfc ridge \nbuilds in from the north late this week with lows near 40 degrees and\nhighs near 60 degrees.\n\nSeasonable and dry weather continue through the upcoming weekend. A \nbrief warming trend then occurs early next week with a return of \nsoutherly flow ahead of our next front.",
